<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">World's Parliament of Religions</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Parliament of the World's Religions (1893)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The World's Parliament of Religions was a gathering of representatives of the world's religious traditions held in Chicago from 11 to 27 September 1893, alongside the World's Columbian Exposition. Often regarded as the beginning of formal dialogue between religions, it gave many Americans their first encounter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, above all through the addresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a> and the Buddhist teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagarika-dharmapala">Anagarika Dharmapala</a>. A modern Parliament of the World's Religions has met since its centenary in 1993.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ram Mohan Roy founded the Brahmo Samaj in 1828 to promote a rational, monotheistic Hinduism; Dayananda Saraswati founded the Arya Samaj in 1875 to return to the Vedas; and Swami Vivekananda’s address to the <b>World’s Parliament of Religions</b> in Chicago in 1893 introduced Hindu philosophy to Western audiences.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
